#!/usr/bin/python3
# umik-sdr - scan-and-capture recorder for an RTL-SDR dongle (Nooelec NESDR
# SMArt v5, or any RTL2832U stick on USB 0bda:2838 or 0bda:2832).
#
# Every pass, from service start until it is stopped:
#   1. scan     rtl_power sweeps the main range (108-1750 MHz) and the fine
#               ranges (FM band, 136-174 and 400-520 MHz), UMIK_SDR_SWEEPS
#               times each;
#   2. choose   signals standing clear of the local noise floor, clustered so
#               that one transmitter is one channel, plus the channels
#               remembered from recent passes; strongest first, capped;
#   3. capture  each channel as raw IQ with rtl_sdr, in shuffled order, with
#               a random gap before each one and a jittered dwell;
#   4. pause    for a random while, then the next pass.
# Nothing is demodulated here: the Pi records, the Mac analyses.
#
# Design notes:
#   * IQ, not audio. Demodulating in the field means guessing what the
#     transmitter is (AM? NBFM? data?) and throwing every other answer away.
#     Raw IQ keeps them all, at 2 bytes per sample ("cu8", rtl_sdr's own
#     format). The price is disk: ~31 MB per 15 s clip at the wide rate,
#     which only the FM band gets (iq_rate_for).
#   * Every clip is a SigMF recording (sigmf.org, v1.0.0): the bytes exactly
#     as rtl_sdr wrote them in <base>.sigmf-data, and <base>.sigmf-meta
#     beside them saying what they are - rate, tuned centre, target, times,
#     clock trust, the scan's evidence, a SHA-512 of the data. Any
#     SigMF-aware tool opens a clip with no help from us.
#   * Offset tuning. An RTL dongle has a spur at the exact centre of whatever
#     it is tuned to, so we tune iq_rate/4 above the target; the target sits
#     at baseband -iq_rate/4, well clear of it.
#   * Two gains, split where the IQ rate splits (gain_for). In the FM band a
#     strong station compresses the tuner above ~20 dB and its skirts read as
#     signals; everywhere else the 8-bit ADC is the limit, and more gain lifts
#     weak carriers out of its quantisation noise. Measured with the SMArt v5
#     (2026-10-10): 15.7 dB for the FM band, 37.2 dB for the rest. The main
#     scan starts at 108 MHz, so the FM band is only ever scanned at its gain.
#   * Everything in the session directory is flat and either complete or a
#     *.part. A clip's data is written as .part, its length checked against
#     what we asked for, fsynced and renamed; then its meta, hashed from the
#     data on disk, goes the same way (.part, fsync, rename); only then is
#     the clip logged in clips.jsonl (appended and fsynced). Pull the power
#     at any moment and the most you lose is the clip in flight: ingest
#     ignores *.part, and a .sigmf-data whose .sigmf-meta never landed is
#     deleted at the next start, like the .part files.
#   * One rtl_power run per sweep. rtl_power averages every sweep that fits
#     into an integration interval into one row per hop, and we need the
#     sweeps apart to tell an always-on carrier from a burst (occupancy).
#     "-i 1 -1" reports after the first sweep that ends a second after the
#     start: exactly one sweep of the ~600-hop main scan, at most a second's
#     average of a short fine scan. Separate runs also let alternate sweeps
#     start a quarter hop off (next note).
#   * DC spur. Each rtl_power hop carries the dongle's DC spur in its centre
#     bin, and its edge bins roll off. The centre bins are masked, and every
#     other sweep is shifted by a quarter hop, so every frequency sits well
#     inside a hop, unmasked, in some sweeps - and a spur that slips past the
#     mask lands somewhere else every other sweep and fails the occupancy
#     test. (Not half a hop: that puts the shifted centres, masked, exactly
#     on the plain hops' edges, rolled off, and a carrier there loses both.)
#   * The hops are not flat: the dongle's filters roll every hop's edges off,
#     the same shape each time. Left in, a weak always-on carrier near a hop
#     edge reads as intermittent and is thrown away. The shape is measured
#     from each sweep's own hops and taken out (hop_profile), rather than
#     cropped away with rtl_power's -c, which costs 25% more hops per sweep
#     (UMIK_SDR_CROP is there if the hardware ever says otherwise).
#   * The schedule is random - shuffled order, random gaps, jittered dwell,
#     random pause between passes - so a transmitter cannot learn when it is
#     being listened to and keep quiet then.
#   * Never trust an rtl_* exit status alone. Both tools print most failures
#     ("dropped samples", "Failed to set center freq", "PLL not locked") and
#     still exit 0, and rtl_sdr can record forever (see capture_samples).
#     Every run is judged by what it printed and how much data it produced.
#   * Standard library only: the Pi OS Lite image has python3 and nothing
#     else, and provisioning is offline.

